
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "About";
    ViewData["Description"] =
        "About Mart Ops, a connected software platform for glazing companies covering office management, surveying, fitting and customer communication.";
}

<div class="container py-4">

    <div class="text-center mb-5">
        <h1 class="display-5 fw-bold">
            About
        </h1>

        <div class="mx-auto mt-4 about-intro">

    <p>
        Mart Ops started as a hobby project at the beginning of 2026.
        The original idea was inspired by a software platform I had
        worked with for many years in the glazing industry, particularly
        the way office-based systems connected with remote workers.
    </p>

    <p>
        What began as an opportunity to revisit those ideas using modern
        .NET technologies gradually developed into a much larger project.
        It became a way to explore how a complete operations
        platform could be designed today, bringing together contract
        management, diary and booking systems, mobile working, surveys,
        fittings, remedials and customer communication.
    </p>

    <p>
        The project now includes a web-based back-office system, mobile
        applications for field operatives and customers, insurer-facing
        functionality and a set of APIs that allow each part of the system
        to work together. It has also provided an opportunity to explore
        areas such as offline working, dynamic questionnaires, media
        capture, multi-tenant architecture and more automated surveying
        workflows.
    </p>

    <p>
        A large part of the project has been about taking ideas that once
        required specialist or legacy systems and rethinking them using
        current technologies such as ASP.NET Core, .NET MAUI, Entity
        Framework Core and modern web interfaces.
    </p>

    <p class="mb-0">
        As the project grew, so did its purpose. Mart Ops is now
        developed primarily as a project of general interest — somewhere
        to experiment with new ideas, learn new technologies, revisit
        long-standing industry problems and continue building upon
        experience gained from many years of commercial software
        development.
    </p>

</div>
    </div>


